86
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:54,460
But if we can just be honest, some of that is the English language is fault.

87
00:05:54,460 --> 00:05:57,900
Like, the English language sometimes can be complicated, difficult.

88
00:05:57,900 --> 00:06:02,280
Not all of the rules always make sense, and sometimes they contradict themselves.

89
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:08,600
So, I might think I'm right and then realize that there's some reason why the rule changes and I'm wrong.

90
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:12,280
And I think a great example of this is homonyms.

91
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:21,780
So, a homonym is two or more words that have the same spelling or pronunciation, but they have different meanings or origins.

92
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:23,100
So, we know this, right?

93
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:26,980
Like, words that are spelled the same or sound the same that have two totally different meanings.

94
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:29,020
I have a couple examples for us.

95
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:30,500
The word rock, right?

96
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:33,420
You could say, let's listen to some rock today.

97
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:37,020
Or you could say, let's go skip a rock today.

98
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:40,280
Same word, two very different meanings.

99
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:41,900
What about the word well?

100
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:44,680
I hope you are feeling well today.

101
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:49,260
Or I hope you're feeling well enough to dig a well today.

102
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:51,980
Like, those are same words, totally different meanings.

103
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:55,300
Last example I have is the word kind.

104
00:06:55,300 --> 00:06:56,840
The word kind.

105
00:06:56,840 --> 00:06:59,540
So, you could say, wow, he is really kind.

106
00:06:59,540 --> 00:07:03,520
Or you could say, wow, he is a real one of a kind.

107
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:07,060
And those two words can mean totally different things.

108
00:07:07,060 --> 00:07:10,920
And sometimes they can even mean opposing things.

109
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:20,240
And the reason that I tie this illustration back to the book of James is because last week we talked about, James talked about trials.

110
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:26,060
And this week you're going to see your text flip and it's going to start talking about temptations.

111
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:28,960
And so, in your English translation, you're going to see two different words.

112
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:34,140
You're going to see trials at the beginning of James and temptations here picking up in verse 13.

113
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:36,840
But the reality is in the Greek, it's the same word.

114
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:40,460
They mean different things based on the context.

115
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:51,620
And so, James is going to say, hey, when trials come, you should face them with joy because trials can produce a positive outcome.

116
00:07:51,620 --> 00:07:56,520
So, last week we said trials, same word as temptations in your English translation.

117
00:07:56,520 --> 00:08:01,260
Trials, you should face with joy because God has a positive outcome.

118
00:08:01,260 --> 00:08:02,500
He has spiritual maturity.

119
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:06,060
Like, we are conformed to the image of Jesus through our trials.

120
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:08,620
This week he's going to transition.

121
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:17,340
He's going to say the same Greek word, temptations, but he's going to say you should avoid temptations because temptations produce a negative outcome.

122
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:23,720
So, in this whole section of James chapter 1, he's talking about the same word meaning two things based on the context.

123
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:26,620
He's saying trials are a good thing.

124
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:29,640
They might not feel good in the moment, but they're good.

125
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:31,460
They produce a good outcome.

126
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:34,800
Spiritual maturity comes through trials.

127
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:40,520
And then he's going to say avoid temptation because there is a negative outcome for temptation.

128
00:08:40,520 --> 00:08:45,640
And that negative outcome is disobedience that leads to destruction.

129
00:08:45,640 --> 00:08:52,900
That's going to be James' whole main point for this passage from verse 12 all the way down to verse 18.

130
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:56,960
He's going to say there is a negative outcome to temptations.

131
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:01,500
And that negative outcome is disobedience that leads to destruction.

132
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:11,060
So, essentially, James' whole point from where we started last week to where we're going to end this week is that believers in Jesus, followers of Christ,

133
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:18,500
we should embrace trials with joy because we should embrace trials with joy because we know that God can produce a positive outcome from them.

134
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:27,980
But we should avoid temptations because ultimately they lead to disobedience that ultimately leads to destruction.

135
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:29,840
So, that's kind of where we're going for the rest of the day.

136
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:32,960
Now, let's read it starting in verse 12.

137
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:38,780
James says, God blesses those who patiently endure testing and temptation.

138
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:45,740
So, good things come out of enduring temptation with joy because spiritual maturity comes through it.

139
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:50,880
Afterwards, they will receive the crown of life that God has promised to those who love him.

140
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:54,620
And remember, when you are being tempted, same word, different meaning.

141
00:09:55,180 --> 00:10:03,640
When you're being tempted, do not say, God is tempting me because God is never tempted to do wrong and he never tempts anyone else.

142
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:04,640
Temptation.

143
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:11,760
Temptation comes not from God but from our own desires which entice us and drag us away.

144
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:16,160
These desires give birth to sinful actions or to disobedience.

145
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:22,740
And when sinful actions or disobedience is allowed to grow, it gives birth to death and destruction.

146
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:25,880
So, believers, don't be misled.

147
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:32,780
Whatever is good and perfect is a gift coming down to us from God our Father who created all the lights in the heavens.

148
00:10:32,780 --> 00:10:37,060
He never changes or casts a shifting shadow.

149
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:40,420
He chose to give birth to us by giving us the true word.

150
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:46,620
And we, out of all creation, have become his prized possession.

151
00:10:46,620 --> 00:10:55,640
So, verse 12, he says, listen, the goal for believers, our goal is to finish our life faithful to God.

152
00:10:55,640 --> 00:11:00,540
Like, to finish our life still following the way of Jesus.

153
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:07,560
He says, those people who patiently endure trials, who patiently endure temptation, they are blessed.

154
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:10,340
They will receive the crown of life.

155
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:15,720
And this crown of life is an illustration that they would have understood in the context of the Roman culture.

156
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:20,080
That wreath that was put on the head of a runner who finished the race.

157
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:24,660
And James is saying, listen, the goal for followers of Jesus is to finish the race.

158
00:11:24,660 --> 00:11:32,680
And our lives are filled with things that will try and get us off track, try and keep us from finishing the race well.

159
00:11:32,680 --> 00:11:36,160
I've recently been talking a lot about the world, the flesh, and the devil.

160
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:46,140
These are three components in this fallen, broken world that are all trying to get us to give up, trying to get us to stop running the race, to stop following Jesus.

161
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:47,120
Life gets hard.

162
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:48,180
Things get difficult.

163
00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:50,560
Trials enter our lives.

164
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:54,240
And James says, no, keep persevering.

165
00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:59,480
Because those who finish the race faithful, they will receive this blessing.

166
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:02,020
They will receive the crown of life.

167
00:12:02,020 --> 00:12:08,840
And so this verse 12 is kind of tying the two paragraphs together, tying trials and temptation together.

168
00:12:08,840 --> 00:12:12,460
He's saying, listen, you will have both trials and temptations.

169
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:17,060
And both of those things can keep you from finishing the race well.

170
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:24,320
But blessed are those who finish the race well, who endure trials and avoid temptations.

171
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:34,220
Blessed are those who persevere trials and avoid temptation.

172
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:39,880
And then he goes on in the next couple of verses and explains why is temptation bad?

173
00:12:39,880 --> 00:12:40,660
How does it work?

174
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:41,640
Why is it bad?

175
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:44,880
Why does it produce a bad outcome?

176
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:49,380
He starts in verse 13 by saying, consider the source.

177
00:12:49,380 --> 00:13:01,380
He says, when you are tempted, whenever you feel temptation drawing you away from God, drawing you into sin, you should know first and foremost that that temptation is not from God.

178
00:13:01,380 --> 00:13:07,720
And James is later in verse 17 going to say that anything good and perfect comes from God.

179
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:14,580
So therefore, if temptation doesn't come from God, we can automatically call it out and say that temptation is not from God.

180
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:15,720
Therefore, it is not good.

181
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:18,440
It has evil intent.

182
00:13:18,440 --> 00:13:20,960
We should consider the source.

183
00:13:20,960 --> 00:13:24,380
Whenever we are tempted, we should be naturally skeptical of that temptation.

184
00:13:24,380 --> 00:13:27,560
We should automatically say, hold on, this temptation isn't from God.

185
00:13:27,560 --> 00:13:29,520
It's trying to lead me away from God.

186
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:32,880
So I should pay attention to it.

187
00:13:34,220 --> 00:13:39,340
Jesus, when he's teaching believers how to pray, the famous Lord's Prayer, right?

188
00:13:39,340 --> 00:13:41,760
He says, lead us not into temptation.

189
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:46,960
Jesus knows that temptation is a negative thing that produces negative outcomes.

190
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:54,080
And so he says, when we pray, we should ask God not to lead us into temptation, that we would be able to avoid temptation.

191
00:13:54,080 --> 00:14:00,900
James says at the forefront of this talk on temptation to consider the source.

192
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:08,940
I don't know if you've ever been in a conversation with someone and they pointed out some strange political thing that you've never heard of before.

193
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:16,100
They're like, did you hear that, you know, the U.S. government is doing this and so we should build a bomb structure in our backyard.

194
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:17,520
Have you guys ever heard something like that?

195
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:19,220
And you're like, oh, like, send me the link.

196
00:14:19,220 --> 00:14:20,300
I didn't hear that.

197
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:20,860
Send me the link.

198
00:14:21,300 --> 00:14:27,020
And then they send you the link and you open it up and you're like, oh, okay, consider the source, right?

199
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:40,420
Like, there are sources on both sides of the political spectrum where you open up the link and you're like, okay, like, I can process this information, but I'm going to process it understanding that this person or this writer is coming from a certain perspective.

200
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:42,380
They have a certain bias.

201
00:14:42,580 --> 00:14:47,500
So James is saying when you're tempted, consider the source, like it's not a good source.

202
00:14:47,500 --> 00:14:49,200
Temptation is not a good source.

203
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:52,540
He says when trials come, consider the source as well.

204
00:14:52,540 --> 00:14:55,880
Like, God can use trials to produce positive outcomes.

205
00:14:57,180 --> 00:15:07,120
But when temptation comes, call it out and know that it's not from God and therefore the goal is not to produce something positive, but to produce something negative.

206
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:13,400
Ultimately, verse 15 tells us that the goal of temptation is to bring about death and destruction in our lives.

207
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:17,560
And far too often at the beginning of temptation, we play with it.

208
00:15:17,560 --> 00:15:23,980
We mess around with it because at the beginning of temptation, it's not so clear that the end result, the end goal is death.

209
00:15:23,980 --> 00:15:26,200
It doesn't seem like it's that big of a deal.

210
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:33,760
It's not the beginning of temptation, but at the end, we ultimately know, James is saying here, that temptation leads to destruction.

211
00:15:33,760 --> 00:15:36,140
So he says, consider the source.

212
00:15:36,140 --> 00:15:37,280
It's not from God.

213
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:41,420
And if it's not from God, then what is the source?

214
00:15:41,420 --> 00:15:48,140
Verse 14, he says, temptation comes from our own desires.

215
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:52,220
The source of temptation is our own desires.

216
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:57,920
Some translations you have might say lust, or the idea behind it is an appetite or a thirst.

217
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:10,520
There is a desire that we have, and ultimately that desire leads us away or deceives us into searching for satisfaction in that desire that comes from something other than God.

218
00:16:11,620 --> 00:16:17,080
So temptation starts with desire.

219
00:16:17,080 --> 00:16:19,700
And temptation in and of itself is not a sin.

